# -*- coding: utf-8 -*-
# Phase 3 narration. Keys are segment ids used by the assembler.
BLOCKS = {
"OPEN": """In nineteen eleven, the federal government ordered every rancher in this county to build a concrete trough and fill it with arsenic.
A hundred and fourteen years later, a man walking a public trail in Ladera Ranch found a concrete base in the ground.
Nobody has ever tested the soil around it. This film is about why not. And about exactly where you would test, if you wanted to know.""",

"P1TITLE": """Part one. The concrete structures.""",

"S2": """A dipping vat is a narrow concrete trough. Twenty-six feet long, six and a half feet deep, with a sloped entry at one end and a ramp at the other. Beside it sits a drip pen. A flat concrete floor, roughened, sloped toward a drain.
Everything else is timber. The rails the cattle brushed against, the chute gates, the plank walkways. Wood rots. Concrete stays.
This is what one looks like now, in Florida, where the state went looking for them. Low walls under scrub. A trench full of reeds. A ramp half buried.
If you did not know, you would walk straight past it.""",

"S3": """So I built a list. A hundred and fifty-nine terms, in seven registers. Every phrase a surveyor, an engineer, an archaeologist or an environmental consultant might reach for when they meet a concrete trough in the brush and have no idea what it is.
Then I ran all of them against four hundred and seventy pages of the environmental record for this land.
Twenty-two of those terms are the correct names. Dip vat. Cattle dip. Plunge vat. Tick eradication. Sodium arsenite.
Not one of them appears. Not once, in four hundred and seventy pages.""",

"S4": """Here is the thing, though. Those same consultants wrote about concrete constantly.
A concrete vault a fuel tank came out of. Two concrete-lined containment sumps at the drum farm. A concrete pad that used to be a laser test pad. Concrete tanks for cooling water at a rocket test stand.
Thirty passages. And every single one names what the concrete was for.
Now here is the sentence they used for open grazing land, repeated almost word for word across the ranch. No evidence of contamination, distressed vegetation, petroleum hydrocarbon staining, illegal dumping, or improper waste storage.
Six things to look for. A rotted post and a length of pipe in the brush is none of them.""",

"S5": """There was one question that could have found it. It is printed in every planning area report, and it reads.
Are there currently, or to the best of your knowledge have there been previously, any pits, ponds, or lagoons located on the property in connection with waste treatment or waste disposal.
A vat is a pit. The answer was no.
And I want to be fair about that answer. Because the same person, two questions earlier, volunteered something nobody asked them to. Years ago, we used to dump oil off the corner of the shop building.
That is not somebody hiding things. That is somebody answering honestly a question about waste disposal. A livestock bath is not waste disposal. Nobody was lying. The question simply did not reach.""",

"S6": """There is one place in this valley where somebody did photograph a concrete structure of ambiguous appearance before an excavator reached it.
A farm on Alipaz Street in San Juan Capistrano. February, two thousand and nine.
The contractor mobilised on the twentieth. Three days later, on the twenty-third, they demolished a concrete pad with a tank containment on it. And before they did, it was photographed, and the caption recorded what it was.
It was the farmer's secondary containment. Not in service. Known, named, written down.
That is the process. Somebody stood in a field, photographed a concrete structure, wrote down what it was, and only then brought in the machine.
It is not what happened at Ladera Ranch.""",

"S7": """There are photographs of Ladera Ranch ground. Sixteen of them, taken on the thirty-first of January, two thousand and two, for an environmental assessment of a school site at Sienna Parkway and O'Neill Drive.
Here is what they show. Bare graded earth. Scraper tracks. A haul road. A drainage basin with a standpipe in it. Construction trailers on the horizon.
Not one permanent structure. No foundation. No wall. No slab.
And the reason is in the report's own sentence. Mass grading began in nineteen ninety-nine, and finished in the summer of two thousand and one. The site walk was seven months later.
The environmental professional arrived after the ground was gone.""",

"S8": """And yet, somebody was supposed to be there.
The Ladera Planned Community Program Text, condition twenty-two, requires County conditions for archaeological and paleontological survey, investigation, and, this is the phrase, observance of grading activities. On every Area Plan. On every tract map. Under Board of Supervisors Resolution seventy-seven, eight sixty-six.
That means a qualified person was contractually required to be standing there while the ground was opened.
Those reports have never been read. They sit at the regional records office at Cal State Fullerton, and nobody in this investigation has asked for them.""",

"S9": """So this is the map of part one. One concrete base, located approximately. Two wells named in the record. Ten anomalies I tested from the air and resolved as drainage, brush and ranch tracks. And a school site where the only photographs of this ground were taken after it had been levelled.
That is everything the record gives us about where to look.
Which brings the obvious question. If you wanted to actually answer this, where would you test?""",

"P2TITLE": """Part two. Where should the soil be tested.""",

"S10": """Before you can decide where to dig, you have to know what happened to the ground.
In nineteen thirty-seven, this was open rangeland. Natural drainage, no field boundaries, nothing worked.
By nineteen fifty-three, look at it. Contour ploughing across the ridgetops and the valley floors. Curved bands following the slope. Field edges.
And somebody wrote on the photograph. A surname. An acreage, two hundred and thirty acres. A year, nineteen forty-five, and a crop. On the next frame, field numbers circled in ink, and the word beans, over and over.
These are not just aerial photographs. Somebody used them as farm maps.
And that matters. Because ploughing mixes the top foot of soil. It does not remove concrete, and it does not cut the profile.""",

"S11": """Then, between nineteen ninety-nine and the summer of two thousand and one, the developed footprint was mass graded.
On the school parcel alone, the report records fill up to a hundred and ten feet deep. Taken from the site itself, and from undeveloped land immediately around it.
And the report says something else, about pesticides, that is worth reading slowly.
Any pesticides or herbicides at the site were likely in the upper surficial soils, which were removed prior to fill placement. These materials, if any, were likely mixed with clean soil, and or placed at lower depths during fill placement.
Read that again. The material is not described as leaving the property. It is described as stripped from the surface, mixed, and put back deeper.
And I have to be careful here. The plans tell us how much earth moved, and how deep. They do not tell us which earth went where.""",

"S12": """So by the time anyone could take a sample, this land came in three conditions.
Ground that was ploughed and then graded. The top foot mixed, then the whole profile cut or buried.
Ground that was ploughed but never graded. Mixed shallow, but still sitting where it always sat.
And ground that was neither. Never farmed, never cut. That is the ground that still holds its original surface. And it is the most informative ground on the map.
I built a map of which ground was worked. It is a sketch, not a survey. The real boundaries are on the grading plans, which I have asked for, and do not yet have.""",

"S13": """Earlier in this investigation I mapped every surface water body recorded on this land by the nineteen sixty-eight survey. And I asked a simple question of each one. If cattle were dipped here, where would they have been dipped?
Not at the house. Not at the road. At the water. A vat needs several thousand gallons on a fortnightly cycle. You build it where the water is.
That produced twenty-three locations. And then reality intervened, because most of them are now under something.
So each one was re-examined against what is there today. Fairway, slope, park, landscape strip, road. And moved to the nearest accessible ground that still relates to the original point.""",

"S14": """And here is the part that decides whether any of this means anything.
Arsenic occurs naturally in California soils. A number on its own tells you nothing. A number only means something next to another number.
So every sample at a historic water point is paired. With undisturbed ground that was never farmed and never graded. And with background soil in the same geology, off the ranch entirely.
If the source areas read the same as the controls, that is an answer. A real one. And it would be the most valuable result this investigation could produce.""",

"S15": """And these are not exotic tests. Surface soil, nought to six inches, where a child's hand reaches. A deeper interval where fill was placed. Digestion, then mass spectrometry. Speciation where total arsenic warrants it, because the form matters more than the amount.
Standard methods. Ordinary laboratories. A few hundred dollars a sample.""",

"P3TITLE": """Part three. The purple pipe loop.""",

"S16": """Before anything else, one distinction. Because everything downstream depends on it.
Ladera Ranch has two water systems.
One is drinking water. Imported, treated, tested to state standards, published every year in a consumer confidence report. That is not what this part of the film is about, and nothing here should be read as a claim about the tap.
The other is the non-potable system. Recycled water, in purple pipes, for landscape irrigation. Slopes, parks, medians, common area turf.
Different pipes. Different storage. Different rules. And a very different testing history.""",

"S17": """Now follow the water.
Rain and irrigation run off roofs, lawns, streets and slopes. It enters the storm system and the Sienna Botanica corridor, and it drains to Horno Creek.
At the bottom of the watershed it collects in the Horno Water Quality Basin, owned and operated by the County of Orange. When a storm is big enough, the excess carries on toward San Juan Creek and the ocean. The loop takes the low flows. The dry weather water.
From the basin it passes through seven acres of constructed wetlands, owned by the Ladera Ranch Homeowners Association.
And then it is pumped into the district's recycled distribution system, and blended with recycled water, for, in the permit's own words, subsequent landscape irrigation use.
Roughly a hundred and eighty acre feet a season. About fifty-nine million gallons. Sprayed back onto the community it came from.""",

"S18": """There is an assumption worth killing here, because it is the natural one to make.
This water does not pass through the Chiquita Water Reclamation Plant.
The permit proves it four ways, and the cleanest is the monitoring scheme. Provision K sets up two separate sampling points. One. Sample the diversion water, either before or after mixing with recycled water. Two. Sample the effluent from the recycled water facilities at the point of discharge, prior to blending with diversion water.
Two monitoring points, for two streams, that meet downstream. That is incoherent if the runoff had already been through the plant.
And the waived parameters say the same thing. The diversion water is exempted from biochemical oxygen demand, total suspended solids, and odour. Sewage plant metrics. The waiver exists because this stream was never sewage.
So the treatment this water receives, in total, is a settling basin and seven acres of wetland. That is the permitted design. It is normal for urban runoff recovery. What makes it worth a film is the history of the land it drains.""",

"S19": """Which brings the question this whole part exists to ask. What has anybody measured in this water?
The permit records characterisation sampling, November two thousand and four through April two thousand and five. Here is the list.
Total dissolved solids. Sulfate. Sodium. Chloride. Boron. Nitrate. Fluoride. Phosphorus.
Salts and nutrients. Every one of them a parameter you measure to protect plants and groundwater from salinity.
Now here is what is not on that list. Arsenic. A pesticide panel. Pharmaceuticals.
I want to be precise about what that means, because it is easy to overstate. It does not mean those things are in the water. It means nobody looked. Twenty years of operation, and the publicly identified testing reviewed in this project does not answer the question.""",

"S20": """Now I have to correct something. Because the obvious story here is wrong, and I would rather say so than sell it.
The obvious story is that a loop concentrates. Water goes round, evaporates, comes back stronger, round again. Compounding.
It does not work. The loop is partial. Storms bypass it. Plants transpire it. Recycled water dilutes it. Model that generously, and the enrichment tops out somewhere around one point zero two to one point two eight times. That is not compounding. That is noise.
So the water concentration story fails.
What survives is different, and simpler. Redistribution. Whatever the drainage carries is sprayed across the community, rather than leaving it. And deposition. Water evaporates, and anything persistent it carried stays in the soil. A little at a time, for two decades and counting.
Those are not the same question. And the second one has never been measured.""",

"S21": """One more thing to be careful about.
This land grew beans and barley for decades, and it is landscaped intensively now. Both of those are reasons to test for pesticides. Neither is evidence that pesticides are circulating.
So the honest formulation is not, pesticides are in Ladera's water. It is this. Given the documented land use, here is the panel a competent laboratory would run, and here is why.""",

"S22": """So how would you actually answer it?
Not with one sample. With a network small enough to fund, and specific enough to tell sources apart.
You need to know what enters the system. What the basin and the wetlands change. What the recycled stream carries on its own. What comes out of the blend. What reaches a sprinkler head. And what, if anything, ends up in the ground.
Seven points. That is the whole design.""",

"S23": """And there it is. The whole investigation, in one frame.
Where the structures might be. Where the water was. Where the earth was moved. Where the drainage collects. Where it is treated. Where it is sprayed. And where you would put a sampler, to find out whether any of it matters.""",

"END": """I want to be clear about what this film has not shown you.
It has not shown you a dipping vat. It has not shown you contamination. It has not shown you a single laboratory result. Because there are not any. And that is the finding, not the failure.
What the records do tell us is where to look. The historical documents tell us what this land was, and what the people surveying it were never asked. The engineering documents tell us where the water goes, and that it comes back. The grading plans tell us the earth was moved, and how deep.
None of that is proof of anything. All of it is a map.
A hundred and fourteen years ago the state told ranchers here to put arsenic in the ground. And they did it, because the law required it. Nobody was careless. Nobody was hiding anything. The paperwork simply never had a box for what happened next.
Modern laboratory testing can answer the question the historical record cannot. A sprinkler head. A sediment core. A patch of long irrigated turf, and a patch of ground that was never touched. Soil at a handful of old water points, and soil somewhere that never was one.
That is not an accusation. It is an experiment. And it is a small one.
The records tell us where to look. Someone should look.""",
}

